"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was 4.7, and the surgical pathology revealed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) with positive surgical margins and extraprostatic extension, which are adverse findings. Conversely, the seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. The biopsy showed a lower grade (Gleason 3+3, ISUP grade group 1) compared to the prostatectomy specimen, which may suggest a difference in tumor biology. The absence of lymph node removal and the lack of nodal sampling introduce uncertainty, as pNx is not equivalent to pN0. The primary uncertainty regarding future prognosis lies in the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory, as this information is currently missing. A postoperative PSA measurement and its trend would significantly revise the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 66.41 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 43.16–89.65 months; its coverage has not been validated."
